# Security Policy

## Report a Vulnerability

Report a vulnerability in {{ project_name }} privately. Never put it in a public issue, {{ pr }} or discussion: that tells attackers about it before a fix exists.

On the repository's **Security** tab, choose **Report a vulnerability**. The report reaches the maintainers only, and the fix is discussed in a private security advisory until it is released. If the tab has no such button, open an issue that asks for a private contact, and leave out every detail of the vulnerability.

Include:

- what is affected: the {% if library %}package and its version{% else %}version or commit{% endif %}, and the file, endpoint or feature;
- how to reproduce it, as steps or a proof of concept;
- what it lets an attacker do;
- any fix or mitigation you know of, and how you'd like to be credited.

## What to Expect

- An acknowledgement of the report within 14 days, then whether it is confirmed.
- A fix developed in private and released as soon as it is ready, with an entry under `### Security` in the changelog.
- Disclosure coordinated with you: the security advisory is published once the fix is released. Keep the details private until then.
- Credit for the report in the advisory, unless you'd rather not be named.

## Supported Versions
{% if library %}
Fixes ship as a patch of the latest minor version of the current major version. Before 1.0.0, only the latest release gets fixes.

| Version | Fixes |
| --- | --- |
| The latest minor of the current major, such as 2.3.x while 2.3 is the newest minor | Yes |
| Every older version | No |

## Verify a Package

The packages are built in CI from a tagged commit of this repository, deterministically and with Source Link.
{%- if not publisher %} Once the one-time setup that `.github/workflows/publish.yml` describes in its header is done, they reach nuget.org only through that workflow, which publishes them from a published GitHub release through nuget.org trusted publishing: no long-lived API key exists, and each package's version is its release's tag.
{%- elif publisher %} They reach nuget.org through this repository's own workflow, `{{ publisher }}`.
{%- endif %}

- Each package's PDB maps every source file to its commit in this repository, and its symbol package (`.snupkg`) is on the nuget.org symbol server. With Source Link and the nuget.org symbol server turned on in your debugger, you step into the exact source a package was built from. NuGet Package Explorer shows whether a package's Source Link and deterministic build are valid.
- nuget.org signs every package it accepts: `dotnet nuget verify --all <package>.nupkg` checks that signature.
{%- if not publisher %}
- Each release carries build provenance: `gh attestation verify <package>.nupkg --repo OWNER/REPO` checks that a package was built by this repository's workflows from the tagged commit. Replace OWNER/REPO with this repository's path.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
Fixes go into the default branch and the next release. Only the latest release is supported: update to it to get a fix.
{%- endif %}
{%- if not library or release_policy != 'none' %}

## Verify a Release
{%- if not library %}

Releases are immutable: once one is published, its tag and files can't change, and GitHub signs an attestation of them. `gh release verify <tag> --repo OWNER/REPO` checks a release's attestation, and `gh release verify-asset <tag> <file> --repo OWNER/REPO` checks a file downloaded from it. Replace OWNER/REPO with this repository's path.
{%- endif %}
{%- if release_policy != 'none' %}

While the repository is public, the Tag and publish release workflow signs each release tag with [gitsign](https://github.com/sigstore/gitsign), through Sigstore's keyless signing, so no signing key exists to leak. With gitsign installed, check a tag in a clone of the repository, replacing OWNER/REPO with its path:

```shell
gitsign verify-tag --certificate-identity https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/.github/workflows/tag-release.yml@refs/heads/main --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com <tag>
```
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Scope

In scope: the code, build and CI configuration in this repository{% if library %} and the packages built from it{% endif %}, including a vulnerability that comes from how {{ project_name }} uses a dependency.

Out of scope: a vulnerability in a dependency itself. Report it to that project. Once it publishes a fix, an issue or {{ pr }} here that moves to the fixed version is welcome, though Dependabot usually proposes it first.
